      "type": "text",
      "text": "```json\n{\"unit_id\": \"Minemaes\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"C1 Mnw-ms.w is the god Min (Mnw) plus ms.w 'born', rendered Min-mesu. The consonants match: Minemaes has m-n-m-s, the weak w can go unwritten and the vowels are conventional. But matching consonants are not enough. Read as a modern spelling, Minemaes does not render Min + ms. Dodson & Hilton render the verb ms as -mose or -mes(su), as in Ahmose and Ramesses. In their spellings, 'em' after a god's name renders the preposition m 'in', as in Amenemhat and Amenemopet. A final '-aes' with its 'a' renders an ayin or aleph followed by the suffix =s 'her', as in Senebhenaes. So Minemaes reads as Min + em + a(e)s, a name built on a preposition and a separate word. That is not Min + ms.w, so record 1 is a different name from C1. Review X treats the 'em' and 'ae' vowels as filler and misses this. C2 Jmn-ms.w is the god Amun plus ms.w, rendered Amun-mesu. The 'Mine-' of record 1 renders Min, not Amun (Amen-/Amun-), and record 1 also fails to read as ms.w, for the same reason as C1. So C2 is not the same name. Neither candidate is the same name as record 1.\"}\n```"
